/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  THE CONVERGENCE CONTRACT, DECLARED.

  This case declares the numbers its reactive-equilibrium solve is judged by
  instead of inheriting them.  They are exactly the documented defaults, and
  declaring them is still not redundant: a case that names its tolerance says
  the author chose it, and the run reports `(system/solverDict)` instead of
  `(defaults)` so a reader can tell the two apart.

      tolerance   absolute test on the NORMALIZED residual
      relTol      relative-reduction test; 0 DISABLES it (OpenFOAM's own
                  convention -- it does not mean "require exact zero")
      maxIter     iteration cap; reaching it with neither test satisfied is
                  NOT convergence, and the refusal names the criterion

  WHAT IS NORMALIZED, AND AGAINST WHAT.  The outer Newton balances, per
  volatile, ln a_i(liquid) against ln(K_i p_i).  The residual is the
  difference; the NORMALIZATION divides the summed residual by the spread of
  those terms about their own mean -- OpenFOAM's `normFactor` with cells
  replaced by equations.  For this case the factor is about 7.4, so the
  reported normalized residual is roughly an eighth of the raw one.  Both are
  printed, every run.  Contract and its honest limits: `src/solver/
  Convergence.H`; the ruling: `docs/design/normalized-residual-convergence.md`.

  An unknown key inside the block is REFUSED by name.  A declared control the
  solver does not implement is not a decoration; it is an instruction the
  author believes is in force.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

reactiveEquilibrium
{
    tolerance   1e-9;
    relTol      0;
    maxIter     60;
}
